<section class="home-section home-editorial-section home-approach-section" aria-labelledby="home-approach-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Approach</p><h2 id="home-approach-title">From a difficult question to a defensible result</h2><p class="section-intro">I work from problem formulation through modelling, implementation and validation to clear communication. The standard is consistent across the work: make assumptions visible, build reproducibly, test what the evidence supports and explain the result clearly.</p></div><a class="section-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/about.html">About how I work →</a></div><div class="about-capabilities" aria-label="How I work"><article><span>01</span><h3>Frame the problem</h3><p>Turn an open-ended question into assumptions, objectives, constraints and measurable outcomes.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Build and test</h3><p>Translate mathematical and statistical ideas into models, algorithms and reproducible computational experiments.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Explain clearly</h3><p>Communicate the substance for technical, professional and wider audiences without hiding assumptions or unnecessary complexity.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Lead collaboratively</h3><p>Coordinate programmes and projects, mentor researchers, build collaborations and connect people with different expertise.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="home-section home-notes-section home-editorial-section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Notes</p><h2>Selected notes</h2><p class="section-intro">Practical writing on AI-assisted technical work, scope, verification and agent workflows.</p></div><a class="section-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes.html">View all notes →</a></div><div class="home-notes-list">
<article class="home-note-row"><a class="home-note-row-visual" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/how-i-use-ai.html" aria-label="Read How I use AI in technical work"><img src="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/img/notes/ai-agent-stack.svg" alt="A five-layer diagram of an AI agent workflow, from model to human review"></a><div class="home-note-row-copy"><div class="home-note-row-meta"><time>30 July 2026</time><span>AI · Workflow · Research</span></div><h3><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/how-i-use-ai.html">How I use AI in technical work</a></h3><p>A practical account of how I use AI across research, scientific computing and technical communication: where it adds leverage, where I retain control, and how I verify what matters.</p><a class="home-note-row-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/how-i-use-ai.html">Read note <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article>
<article class="home-note-row"><a class="home-note-row-visual" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/ai-real-project-lessons.html" aria-label="Read What I Learned From Letting AI Work on a Real Project"><img src="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/img/notes/scope-control.svg" alt="A bounded technical task contrasted with expanding scope and complexity"></a><div class="home-note-row-copy"><div class="home-note-row-meta"><time>30 July 2026</time><span>AI · Workflow · Research</span></div><h3><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/ai-real-project-lessons.html">What I Learned From Letting AI Work on a Real Project</a></h3><p>A practical framework for using AI on serious technical work without losing control of the problem.</p><a class="home-note-row-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/ai-real-project-lessons.html">Read note <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article>
<article class="home-note-row"><a class="home-note-row-visual" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/ai-agents-practical-stack-2026-qwen9-128k-copilot-opencode-no-gemini-free.html" aria-label="Read From Chatbots to Agents: How I Built My AI Stack in 2026"><img src="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/img/notes/agent-stack-evolution.svg" alt="A progression from chatbot to coding assistant, agent, workflow and automation"></a><div class="home-note-row-copy"><div class="home-note-row-meta"><time>26 July 2026</time><span>AI · Tools · Workflow</span></div><h3><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/ai-agents-practical-stack-2026-qwen9-128k-copilot-opencode-no-gemini-free.html">From Chatbots to Agents: How I Built My AI Stack in 2026</a></h3><p>A practical account of how I moved from using ChatGPT as a chatbot to building a working stack around Codex, OpenCode, DeepSeek, OpenClaw and automation.</p><a class="home-note-row-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/notes/ai-agents-practical-stack-2026-qwen9-128k-copilot-opencode-no-gemini-free.html">Read note <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="home-section selected-publications-section home-editorial-section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Publications</p><h2>Research outputs</h2><p class="section-intro">Selected work spanning optimisation, statistical modelling, inverse problems and applied mathematics.</p></div><a class="section-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/publications.html">View all publications →</a></div><div class="home-publications-list">
<article class="home-publication-row"><div class="publication-theme-rail"><span>Statistical modelling</span><span>Sports analytics</span></div><div class="home-publication-main"><h3>Benchmarking Formula 1 results using a normal model</h3><div class="paper-meta"><span>John Fry, <u>Silvio Fanzon</u>, Mark Austin and Tom Brighton</span><span><em>ArXiv Preprint</em> · 2026</span></div><div class="paper-actions"><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/journal/2026-Fry-Fan-Aus-Bri.pdf">PDF</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15192">arXiv</a><details><summary>Abstract</summary><p>There is enduring interest in disentangling the effects of skill and luck in sport. A key issue in Formula 1 is distinguishing between car-level and driver-level effects. Four elite teams currently dominate Formula 1 and have won every major race for the last four years.</p></details><details><summary>Cite</summary><pre><code>@article&#123;2026-Fry-Fan-Aus-Bri, title = &#123;Benchmarking Formula 1 results using a normal model&#125;, author = &#123;Fry, John and Fanzon, Silvio and Austin, Mark and Brighton, Tom&#125;, year = &#123;2026&#125;&#125;</code></pre></details></div></div></article>
<article class="home-publication-row"><div class="publication-theme-rail"><span>Optimisation</span><span>Numerical algorithms</span></div><div class="home-publication-main"><h3>Asymptotic linear convergence of Fully–Corrective Generalized Conditional Gradient methods</h3><div class="paper-meta"><span>Kristian Bredies, Marcello Carioni, <u>Silvio Fanzon</u> and Daniel Walter</span><span><em>Mathematical Programming</em> · 2024</span></div><div class="paper-actions"><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/journal/2024-Bre-Car-Fan-Wal.pdf">PDF</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10107-023-01975-z">Journal</a><details><summary>Abstract</summary><p>We propose a fully-corrective generalized conditional gradient method for the minimization of a smooth convex loss and a convex one-homogeneous regularizer over a Banach space. Under standard hypotheses we show that the algorithm converges sublinearly, subsequently improving this to a linear rate.</p></details><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/projects/sparse-gcg/index.html">Explainer</a><a href="https://github.com/sfanzon/sparse-gcg-paper-explainer">Code</a><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/journal/slides/2024-Bre-Car-Fan-Wal-Slides-Short.pdf">Slides</a></div></div></article>
<article class="home-publication-row"><div class="publication-theme-rail"><span>Statistical modelling</span><span>Sports analytics</span></div><div class="home-publication-main"><h3>Faster identification of faster Formula 1 drivers via time-rank duality</h3><div class="paper-meta"><span>John Fry, Tom Brighton and <u>Silvio Fanzon</u></span><span><em>Economics Letters</em> · 2024</span></div><div class="paper-actions"><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/journal/2024-Fry-Bri-Fan.pdf">PDF</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.econlet.2024.111671">Journal</a><details><summary>Abstract</summary><p>Two natural ways of modelling Formula 1 race outcomes are a probabilistic approach, based on the exponential distribution, and econometric modelling of the ranks. Both approaches lead to exactly soluble race-winning probabilities.</p></details><a href="/projects/f1-time-rank-duality/">Explainer</a><a href="https://github.com/sfanzon/F1-Paper-Code">Code</a></div></div></article>
<article class="home-publication-row"><div class="publication-theme-rail"><span>Inverse problems</span><span>Optimal transport</span></div><div class="home-publication-main"><h3>A Generalized Conditional Gradient Method for Dynamic Inverse Problems with Optimal Transport Regularization</h3><div class="paper-meta"><span>Kristian Bredies, Marcello Carioni, <u>Silvio Fanzon</u> and Francisco Romero</span><span><em>Foundations of Computational Mathematics</em> · 2023</span></div><div class="paper-actions"><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/journal/2023-Bre-Car-Fan-Rom.pdf">PDF</a><a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10208-022-09561-z">Journal</a><details><summary>Abstract</summary><p>We develop a dynamic generalized conditional gradient method for dynamic inverse problems with optimal transport regularization. We propose a dynamic generalization and prove sublinear convergence.</p></details><a href="https://github.com/sfanzon/DGCG_algorithm">Code</a><a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/journal/poster/2023-Bre-Car-Fan-Rom-Poster.pdf">Poster</a><a href="https://youtu.be/wO8j1fSpd-k">Video</a></div></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="home-section news-preview home-editorial-section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">News</p><h2>Latest updates</h2><p class="section-intro">Recent updates on research, publications, software, teaching and professional activity.</p></div><a class="section-link" href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/news.html">View all news →</a></div><div class="news-list">
<details class="news-item"><summary><time>Mar 2026</time><span>PREPRINT</span><strong>Formula 1 benchmarking preprint released</strong><i aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div><p>Our new paper, <a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/publications.html#2026-Fry-Fan-Aus-Bri">Benchmarking Formula 1 results using a normal model</a>, is now available as a preprint. A long-standing question in sport is how to separate the effects of skill and luck.</p></div></details>
<details class="news-item"><summary><time>Feb 2026</time><span>TALK</span><strong>Presenting at the University of Hull Research Sandpit</strong><i aria-hidden="true"></i></summary><div><p>On 11th February, I will be taking part in the Annual Research Sandpit Event hosted by the School of Digital and Physical Sciences at the University of Hull. My slides are available <a href="https://www.silviofanzon.com/assets/pdf/seminars/slides/2026/2026-Hull-Research-Event-Slides.pdf">here</a>.</p></div></details>
